{"decisions":[{"i":0,"decision":"F0","origin":"none","reason":"In context, the title refers to ending a government-operated signal feature, not requiring users to install a change; this is a reasonable reading of the source.","sourceEvidence":"In May 2000 the US government stopped using Selective Availability; it deliberately degraded public GPS signals.","synthesisStatement":"The synthesis distinguishes ending use of the feature from specifying its omission in a future generation.","narrativeStatement":"The title says users did not have to install the GPS change.","materiality":"No material discrepancy established."},{"i":1,"decision":"F0","origin":"none","reason":"The title is consistent with the article’s account of the government operationally stopping use of the feature, followed by a separate future procurement decision.","sourceEvidence":"The source describes the government stopping use of Selective Availability in 2000 and later announcing future GPS III procurement without it.","synthesisStatement":"The 2000 operational change and 2007 procurement decision are distinct.","narrativeStatement":"The title says users did not have to install the GPS change.","materiality":"No material discrepancy established."},{"i":2,"decision":"F0","origin":"none","reason":"The added sentence makes explicit a reasonable implication of the described operational change; it does not conflate that change with the later procurement decision.","sourceEvidence":"The source says the US government stopped using the signal-degrading feature in 2000; it does not describe a user-side installation.","synthesisStatement":"The synthesis distinguishes the 2000 operational switch from the 2007 procurement decision.","narrativeStatement":"The article calls the 2000 change operational, not an update users had to install.","materiality":"No material discrepancy established."},{"i":3,"decision":"F0","origin":"none","reason":"In context, this describes the government’s operational switch rather than asserting a separate technical detail about users or devices.","sourceEvidence":"The source says the US government stopped using Selective Availability in May 2000.","synthesisStatement":"The 2000 event was an operational switch; the 2007 event concerned future procurement.","narrativeStatement":"This was an operational change, not an update users had to install.","materiality":"No material discrepancy established."}]}

